#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166680 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "dad3d0a2-26e0-52c4-9e5a-abe6664cc02c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8616" :question/problem "A fair die has $9$ sides labeled with numbers $1$ through $9.$ What is the probability of getting a number greater than or equal to $6$ on the next throw of the die?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number the die lands on. From the problem statement, we know that $X \\sim U \\{ 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X \\geq 6).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/04/04a4d9a4cad75503f6dead488d088b95d9e44cde6ca44e73a33cb4e5a242111d.svg)\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim U \\{ 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\},$ and the set $S = \\{ 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\}$ has $|S| = 9-1 + 1 = 9$ items. So, we have\n\n![](images/c3/c34845ee8e867275c17efed55359d7fea4414e2688c25356b28df73c48c974f9.svg)\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X \\geq 6$ are $6, 7, 8, 9.$ Since there are $4$ elements in total, we have\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 6) = 4 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166687 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "de4ae41e-c981-5b5e-b52c-c8e7caf4c272" :question/math-academy-id "e-8647" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The amount of time, in seconds, needed to download a video.\n\nII. The number obtained when rolling a fair $4$-sided die whose sides are labeled with the numbers $5, 6, 7, 8.$\n\nIII. The number of rainy days in a randomly selected summer month in New York City." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each given random variable.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because an amount of time is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of sides, and since the die is fair, each integer between $5$ and $8$ has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because each possible number of rainy days is not equally likely. For example, it's more likely that there will be $5$ rainy days than $31$ rainy days.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e3e30ef-68e6-519f-85f4-fee8d10f81e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424336} {:answer/id #uuid "61ef3bcd-05c5-505d-b1b7-15232aa92580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424341} {:answer/id #uuid "6d59c617-fb6d-55c2-8065-4b569c87d39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424345} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e4752b-5097-5dca-a4d5-bd8632d6c5c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424353} {:answer/id #uuid "c4370a56-f375-577d-973d-adc95a739c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d59c617-fb6d-55c2-8065-4b569c87d39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424345} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e981e36-68c1-59b6-8484-c1aaf524b056" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78d51529-5603-5294-836c-698a99289cb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-148394" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking an integer between $0$ and $10$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a real number between $5$ and $9$ at random.\n\nIII. The number of clients arriving at a shopping mall in a day." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of integers between $0$ and $10,$ and since the selection is random, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many real numbers between $5$ and $9,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of clients arriving at a shopping mall can be any positive integer, and each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5e3c03-d706-5575-856d-866eb3d9500c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424333} {:answer/id #uuid "3c08f617-d8ee-596f-b133-64ab4350a77c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424335} {:answer/id #uuid "67067611-aa63-5326-8173-b77d14f9f6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424342} {:answer/id #uuid "dabb82bc-b608-55c9-b787-5af6495f857a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424359} {:answer/id #uuid "fc726ac5-a2c5-55ad-81bd-c76c122c6b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c08f617-d8ee-596f-b133-64ab4350a77c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424335} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8df995d-74a1-505c-a3a5-e60115444fb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e66fdc5b-c55e-53fe-9d08-47c56ef73570" :question/math-academy-id "q-148406" :question/problem "The only available spaces in a multi-story parking lot are on the second floor, numbered from $215$ to $268.$\nIf Emma parks in one of the spaces at random, what is the probability that she parks in a space which is numbered between $234$ and $245$ inclusive?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the parking space Emma picks. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{215,216,…,268\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(234\\le X\\le 245).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{215,216,…,268\\},$ and the set $S=\\{215,216,…,268\\}$ has $|S|=268-215+1=54$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{54}, & x=215,216,…,268 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $234\\le X\\le 245$ are $234,235,…,245.$ There are $245-234+1=12$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(234\\le X\\le 245)=12\\cdot \\frac{1}{54}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15086c87-9c13-579c-ba6e-748eaf611b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424334} {:answer/id #uuid "69e75bc6-66fd-55e9-bf42-99525bc872c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424343} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec6426d-a527-5edf-abdc-752ce809d846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424346} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcc0a75-c778-5ff4-bb53-0a28275cff60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424347} {:answer/id #uuid "cf03c069-f7d1-542b-9880-50a243a3e4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69e75bc6-66fd-55e9-bf42-99525bc872c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424343} :answer-field/id #uuid "24d10336-4f92-5c3b-8f87-5930254510c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a490336d-2a2e-5374-a15f-1401e77380a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-148408" :question/problem "When rolling a fair six-sided die, what is the probability of getting a prime number?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number on which the die lands. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{1,2,…,6\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\in \\{2,3,5\\}).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,…,6\\},$ and the set $S=\\{1,2,…,6\\}$ has $|S|=6$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}, & x=1,2,…,6 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\in \\{2,3,5\\}$ are $2,3,5.$ There are $3$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\in \\{2,3,5\\})=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2473833-c71c-5186-a12b-67e836950352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166287} {:answer/id #uuid "befa16f4-bc6b-5fcc-b6dd-c4610badece3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166288} {:answer/id #uuid "dbefadaf-3bb5-5096-8c7b-3d6a8d4522fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166289} {:answer/id #uuid "3b938956-23cc-55fc-b602-bb736ae6f44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166290} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff78f82-6395-50f5-b932-406234d8abcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "befa16f4-bc6b-5fcc-b6dd-c4610badece3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166288} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bc777b2-6887-5558-adfa-4aa52867398b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "200aef1f-7404-50e8-879d-3c216fa12872" :question/math-academy-id "q-97569" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking an integer between $1$ and $10$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a real number between $1$ and $10$ at random.\n\nIII. The color of the ball obtained when a ball from a bag containing $3$ blue and $4$ red balls is chosen at random." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of integers between $1$ and $10,$ and since the selection is made at random, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many real numbers between $1$ and $10,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of selecting a red ball.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5547a59d-4082-5471-8b71-d59a07d32d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424339} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0f2fd4-cce9-5b1f-a0fc-bbb4de04e32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424344} {:answer/id #uuid "b6415cf2-9dee-5ed8-8c70-9e39a6b7b363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424355} {:answer/id #uuid "cf10babe-4fb2-5ed6-b0e3-51c161bbe192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424358} {:answer/id #uuid "f912b078-5c68-5e79-9616-1cc70dfbee36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b0f2fd4-cce9-5b1f-a0fc-bbb4de04e32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424344} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9a04253-21f8-57fa-9da2-eb6b04eff7f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f759cee-8f8e-57dc-b752-40d429fccaa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97647" :question/problem "A child is playing with a tablet. The apps on the tablet are labeled with numbers $4$ through $21.$ If the child chooses an app at random, what is the probability that the app number they pick is more than $16?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number of the app picked. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{4,5,…,21\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X>16).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{4,5,…,21\\},$ and the set $S=\\{4,5,…,21\\}$ has $|S|=21-4+1=18$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}, & x=4,5,…,21 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X>16$ are $17,18,19,20,21.$ There are $5$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>16)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}=\\frac{5}{18}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "820ae2a2-894d-5114-98c1-0c069a04fb00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424348} {:answer/id #uuid "90fcb279-3814-5dab-a9da-34cb34cf2297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424351} {:answer/id #uuid "a2517864-8ff2-5d85-b806-1c79fc85fd80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424352} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc11d3d-3403-5323-a459-1449e6d907e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424360} {:answer/id #uuid "e3430a41-06ca-5f20-8875-54682b73bce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90fcb279-3814-5dab-a9da-34cb34cf2297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424351} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77545cc-1b3a-5e85-b752-ea1b743f1c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "574dc243-2e55-5ce7-9e9d-a12e501cd5c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-97660" :question/problem "Jack wants to buy a single issue of a particular comic series with $80$ issues. Unfortunately, the comic store has neither the first $3$ issues nor the final issue in stock. If Jack buys a comic at random from the available issues, what is the probability that he chooses either issue $20$ or issue $23?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the issue Jack selects. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{4,5,…,79\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X=20\\,\\,or\\,\\,X=23).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{4,5,…,79\\},$ and the set $S=\\{4,5,…,79\\}$ has $|S|=79-4+1=76$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{76}, & x=4,5,…,79 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy the given condition are $20,23.$ There are $2$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=20\\,\\,or\\,\\,X=23)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{76}=\\frac{1}{38}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc3d191-cba3-52f4-a11c-dde3db0a56cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{57}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166293} {:answer/id #uuid "1af8addf-ed0d-5b82-873f-cf8c7cc8d138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{56}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166294} {:answer/id #uuid "21102de4-1d55-5a18-bc2c-327303bd4608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{56}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166295} {:answer/id #uuid "73dc79c3-61d5-5a44-b9d7-241c2f32a3dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{57}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166296} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1fe4dc-1393-5f42-aead-cb877bdac66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b1fe4dc-1393-5f42-aead-cb877bdac66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166297} :answer-field/id #uuid "2677ed19-d317-5fac-9ef2-ef42544595ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29f6e184-5ce2-54d0-991f-fb9cc535577d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97691" :question/problem "In a videogame, the number of obstacles that show up during a game can vary between $100$ and $155,$ inclusive. Assuming that each number of obstacles has the same probability to occur, what is the probability of having less than $130$ obstacles?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number of obstacles in a randomly selected game. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{100,101,…,155\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<130).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{100,101,…,155\\},$ and the set $S=\\{100,101,…,155\\}$ has $|S|=155-100+1=56$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{56}, & x=100,101,…,155 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<130$ are $100,101,…,129.$ There are $30$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<130)=30\\cdot \\frac{1}{56}=\\frac{15}{28}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "845c09e6-5d17-5bb5-adbc-3721e754fcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166305} {:answer/id #uuid "b42d62fd-5de8-57e7-89e2-4a835c65eec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166306} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bac43d-fbd2-555d-98fa-4f3cf841dda7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166307} {:answer/id #uuid "ece24b1c-2340-5941-9ac1-1dce184ab664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166308} {:answer/id #uuid "52958bf7-76a5-59bb-8124-ef40c1e1ee10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52958bf7-76a5-59bb-8124-ef40c1e1ee10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166309} :answer-field/id #uuid "648b28e8-61ba-5183-92e3-64a3e48338c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3f94f8a-9ac9-561e-91b8-f484694ecc10" :question/math-academy-id "q-97745" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number of correct answers in a multiple choice test of $10$ questions, where each answer is either \"true\" or \"false,\" and each answer is guessed randomly.\n\nII. The number obtained when spinning a fair roulette wheel.\n\nIII. The initial letter of a word picked at random from an English dictionary." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each number of correct answers is not equally likely. For example, it is more likely to score $50%$ than $100%.$\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. For example, each outcome of a roulette wheel is represented by a number between $0$ and $37,$ and since the roulette is fair, each number is equally likely.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because some letters are more common than others.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e218fff-12f9-5fa5-922d-87fcb0d3466e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424332} {:answer/id #uuid "59a9fa70-556c-52b8-b78c-ec478f8ef8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424340} {:answer/id #uuid "8b023963-20ab-5f85-8fbc-5c210e36192b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424349} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed4e290-9d27-5fac-8fe0-eb7027b7fb73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424350} {:answer/id #uuid "b51a8979-70c9-5fd8-88fb-2353345ae803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e218fff-12f9-5fa5-922d-87fcb0d3466e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424332} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d297b53-634d-5917-86bf-fd5c5bf44384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc3edf0b-9469-5401-acc3-594a44dac66e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97753" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking a card from a set of cards numbered from $1$ to $12,$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when throwing a standard die that is biased toward landing on $1.$\n\nIII. The color obtained when a ball from a bag containing $5$ balls with distinct colors is chosen randomly." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of cards, and since the selection is made at random, each card has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Since the die is not fair, each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of getting $1.$\n\n- Random variable III can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of distinct colors, and since the selection is made at random, each color has an equal probability of being picked.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46101349-6d6a-5bb0-9207-0aaec4554d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424337} {:answer/id #uuid "49dca3bb-2186-5f51-be14-255602c25375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424338} {:answer/id #uuid "f32cc730-85a1-5c7e-be94-acff281af5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424362} {:answer/id #uuid "f4459918-d225-5b1f-98b0-e5d6e8d3db04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424363} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b18ca0-cc56-5a67-b890-09a9290c08a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f32cc730-85a1-5c7e-be94-acff281af5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424362} :answer-field/id #uuid "0328f54f-4ff1-538a-9504-a91aff1cf880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "239e34ce-3c73-5bae-b143-3d41bff8bf51" :question/math-academy-id "q-97754" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The temperature of a town on a certain day.\n\nII. The number of letters of a word randomly selected from an English dictionary.\n\nIII. The number of typos in a randomly selected book." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because a temperature is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely (there are more short words than very long words, for example).\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of typos can be any positive integer. Moreover, each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}]}
